{{smaller block|{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 900.}}}}
{{smaller block|Learning and art, and especially religion, weave ties that make ''war'' look like fratricide, as it is.
{{right|''Emerson'', War.}}}}
'''2.''' A state of active opposition, hostility, or contest: as, to be at ''war'' (that is, engaged in active hostilities).
{{smaller block|<poem>Mine eye and heart are at a mortal ''war.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, xlvi.}}
<poem>A wounded thing with a rancorous cry,
At ''war'' with myself and a wretched race.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Maud, x. 2.}}}}
'''3.''' Any kind of contest or conflict; contention; strife: as, a wordy ''war''.—'''4.''' The profession of arms; the art of war.
{{smaller block|Nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn ''war'' any more. {{right|Isa. ii. 4.}}
<poem>''War'' is our bus’ness, but to whom is giv’n
To die, or triumph, that determine heav’n!</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, xxii. 171.}}}}
'''5.''' Forces; army. Compare ''battle''. [Poetical.]
{{smaller block|<poem>O’er the embattled ranks the waves return
And overwhelm their ''war''. ''Milton'', P. L., xii. 214.</poem>
<poem>In this array the ''war'' of either side
Through Athens passed with military pride.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., iii. 101.}}}}
'''6.''' Warlike outfit.
{{smaller block|<poem>His Complement of Stores, and total ''War''.</poem>
{{right|''Prior'', Henry and Emma.}}}}
{{smaller block|[War is sometimes used in the plural form with the same signification as it has in the singular.
<poem>I’ll to the Tuscan ''wars''. ''Shak.'', All’s Well, ii. 3. 290.]</poem>}}
